question:
What is printed to the console?
```javascript
function asyncOp(callback) {
  setTimeout(() => callback(null, 'result'), 0);
}

asyncOp((err, data) => {
  console.log(data);
});
console.log('start');
```

A Promise is created but never settled. What is the outcome?
```javascript
const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // never calls resolve or reject
});
p.then(() => console.log('resolved'));
console.log('done');
```

What is the output of this Promise chain?
```javascript
Promise.resolve(1)
  .then(x => x + 1)
  .then(x => {
    throw new Error('oops');
  })
  .then(x => console.log('success:', x))
  .catch(e => console.log('caught:', e.message));
```

What does this async/await code do?
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  try {
    const data = await fetch('/api');
    return data.json();
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('error:', e);
  }
}
await fetchData();
```

What is the execution order?
```javascript
async function process() {
  console.log('start');
  await Promise.resolve();
  console.log('after await');
}
process();
console.log('end');
```

Given a generator function, what does yield do?
```javascript
function* gen() {
  const x = yield 1;
  console.log(x);
  yield x + 1;
}
const g = gen();
console.log(g.next());
console.log(g.next(10));
```

A function chains multiple async calls. Which pattern avoids pyramid of doom?
```javascript
// Callback pyramid:
callAsync1((err, data1) => {
  callAsync2(data1, (err, data2) => {
    callAsync3(data2, (err, data3) => {
      console.log(data3);
    });
  });
});
```

In async/await, what happens if you forget await?
```javascript
async function getData() {
  return {id: 1};
}
const result = getData();
console.log(result.id);
```
